**Description**
There are a few types of "statements" that off-chain agents are going to sign:

- Snapshot (Guards, Notaries)
- Attestation (Notaries)
- Report (Guards)

Currently, the signature is obtained by signing an Ethereum Signed Message derived from the hash of statement bytestring (see [EIP-191](https://eips.ethereum.org/EIPS/eip-191))
```solidity
// Pseudocode for "unsafe" signature generation
hashedStatement = keccak("\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\n32" + keccak(statement));
signature = signMessage(privKey, hashedStatement);
```
The problem with this implementation is that it requires no collisions between different types of statements. In other words, a given bytestring could not represent a formatted Snapshot payload and a formatted Attestation payload at the same time.

This is somewhat non-trivial to enforce, but the same thing could be achieved using different salts for hashing different types of statements:
```solidity
SNAPSHOT_SALT = keccak("SNAPSHOT_SALT");
ATTESTATION_SALT = keccak("ATTESTATION_SALT");
hashedSnapshot = keccak("\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\n32" + keccak(SNAPSHOT_SALT + keccak(snapshot)));
hashesAttestation = keccak("\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\n32" + keccak(ATTESTATION_SALT + keccak(attestation)));
```
